\DOC X86_GEN_ACCSTEP_TAC

\TYPE {X86_GEN_ACCSTEP_TAC : tactic -> thm * (thm option array) -> bool -> string -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Symbolically executes one x86 instruction in a goal {A ?- eventually x86 P s},
replacing the assumptions describing the current machine state with those for
the successor state, and optionally recording an arithmetic "accumulator"
equation for the step.

\DESCRIBE
The arguments are {acc_preproc : tactic}, a tactic run as preprocessing while
the step's arithmetic result is being derived; {th : thm * (thm option array)},
a pair of a theorem giving the length of the code (byte list) being executed
and an array of optional per-instruction decoding theorems; {aflag : bool},
saying whether the step is to be treated as an accumulating arithmetic step,
i.e. whether an equation relating the step's output words/carry to its inputs
is introduced; and {s : string}, the base name of the state variable, from
which the current and successor state names are formed. The conclusion is
rewritten for the successor state {s'} (same predicate {P}, with a freshly
numbered state variable), and every assumption mentioning the old state — the
{bytes_loaded}, register, flag and memory {read} assumptions, and the
accumulated {MAYCHANGE ... s0 s} relation — is discharged and replaced by the
corresponding assumption about {s'}: components altered by the instruction get
their new values (the program counter assumption advanced, the {MAYCHANGE}
chain extended with the newly written components), unaffected ones are carried
over unchanged. Pre-existing assumptions not mentioning the state (bounds,
nonoverlapping conditions, earlier arithmetic equations) are kept. One or more
new arithmetic assumptions about the step's result may also be added, e.g. an
equation expressing a product or a sum-with-carry in terms of the input words;
these appear in some applications and not others.
{
         A ?- eventually x86 P s
   ==================================================  X86_GEN_ACCSTEP_TAC acc_preproc th aflag s
         A', e1 ... en ?- eventually x86 P s'
}
\noindent Here {A'} is {A} with the state-dependent assumptions re-expressed
for {s'}, and {e1 ... en} are the possible new result equations (n may be
zero).

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single kind of outcome, always on goals
whose conclusion is an {eventually x86} statement about a named state.

\ENDDOC
